<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zagreb</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zagreb is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> river. It grew from two medieval settlements, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s Kaptol, seat of a bishopric founded in 1094, and the royal free town of Gradec, which were united in 1850. Zagreb was the political centre of Croatia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule and became the capital of independent Croatia in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zagreb.jpg" alt="Zagreb" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nick Savchenko from Kiev, Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zagreb_(29255640143).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zagreb lies in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava River</a>, on the southern slopes of the Medvednica mountain and near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, at an elevation of about 158 metres. Most of its districts lie low in the Sava valley, while the northern and northeastern ones spread across the foothills of Medvednica. Its position, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and Southeast Europe meet, has made it the centre of Croatia's road, rail and air networks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest known settlement near present-day Zagreb was the Roman town of Andautonia, now Šćitarjevo, which existed between the first and fifth centuries CE. The city's own history begins in 1094, when the Hungarian King Ladislaus, returning from a campaign against the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, founded a diocese. Beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s see grew the canonical settlement of Kaptol, north of Zagreb Cathedral, and on the neighbouring hill the fortified settlement of Gradec. The name Zagreb is first recorded in a charter by Felician, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a>.</p><p>Both settlements were attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1242. Béla IV, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, had been given a safe haven in Gradec, and in gratitude granted it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> of 1242, which exempted its citizens from county rule and gave them autonomy and their own judicial system. The name is probably from a Proto-Slavic word meaning a hill or an embankment, perhaps the remains of first-millennium fortifications on Grič.</p><p>In the mid-19th century the Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-jelacic">Josip Jelačić</a>, united Gradec and Kaptol, and the main city square bears his name. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-zagreb">University of Zagreb</a> was founded in 1669. While Croatia was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, from 1918 to 1991, Zagreb was an important economic centre and the second largest city. After Croatia declared independence in 1991 its Parliament proclaimed Zagreb the capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Zagreb is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and the seat of its government, parliament and Constitutional Court, which stand on St. Mark's Square. It has the special status of a city and a county, separate from Zagreb County. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-zagreb">University of Zagreb</a> is the oldest continuously operating university in Croatia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zagreb” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ante Pavelić, a former <b>Zagreb</b> deputy, went into exile in Italy, where Benito Mussolini gave the Ustaše training grounds in exchange for Pavelić's agreement that Dalmatia would go to Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1242 the Mongols plundered Slavonia, sacked <b>Zagreb</b> and attacked Klis, where they failed to take the fortress; afterwards fortresses such as Medvedgrad were built and nobles were allowed to build castles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>As Austria-Hungary collapsed in October 1918, a National Council in <b>Zagreb</b> proclaimed the State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs, which, threatened by Italian advances along the Adriatic, sought rapid union with Serbia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The longest known Etruscan text, probably a ritual calendar, written on a linen book, survives only because the cloth was cut into strips to wrap an Egyptian mummy, now in the Archaeological Museum in <b>Zagreb</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>By the late 16th century Ottoman conquests had reduced Croatia to a narrow band around <b>Zagreb</b>, Karlovac and the coast, the reliquiae reliquiarum of the old kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg">Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)</a></li><li><blockquote>A ban appointed on the Hungarian prime minister's advice headed the government in <b>Zagreb</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia">Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia</a></li></ul></section>
